WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:04.920
Hey guys, it's Jan here.
We all know that Marvel aren't ones to skimp

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:09.389
when it comes to loading their movies and
TV shows with easter eggs and references to

00:00:09.389 --> 00:00:13.440
the wider Marvel universe.
And season 1 of Jessica Jones is certainly

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:18.480
no exception; in fact it's positively laden
with easter eggs and call-backs.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:23.420
Now that all 13 episodes of Jessica Jones
are out on Netflix, I've put together as many

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:26.820
of those eggs and references as I could find
in this here video.

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:31.429
I'm sure you'll have spotted other cool ones
too, so do share those in the comments below.

00:00:31.429 --> 00:00:36.010
And just a quick reminder there will be spoilers
ahead, so if you're not ready for that, check

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:38.649
out my non-spoiler review of the show here!

00:00:38.649 --> 00:00:43.110
The very first scene of the series looks like
it was almost torn straight from the first

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:48.230
issue of the Alias Jessica Jones comics with
Jessica tossing an angry client straight through

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:51.760
the glass pane in the front door of her Alias
Investigations office.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:56.239
The show also makes lots of references to
Jessica's superhero past in the comics, for

00:00:56.239 --> 00:01:00.920
instance when Trish puts forward "Jewel" as
a superhero name and Jessica dismisses it

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:05.260
as a really slutty stripper's name. In the
comics, when Jessica is under Killgrave's

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:10.670
mind control he tells her to put on her slut
costume, in other words her superhero suit.

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:15.400
And the white spandex costume with a purple
jewel that Trish reveals in episode 5 has

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:19.290
obvious similarities to the costume Jessica
wore as Jewel in the comics.

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:24.000
Also, the mask that Trish puts on could be
a nod to Jessica's other comic-book identity

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:29.009
as Knightress for which she wore another costume
and mask, though that mask was a different

00:01:29.009 --> 00:01:33.080
colour to the one Trish wears. And I'll come
back to Trish's desire to be a superhero a

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:36.420
bit later.
Oh, and when Jessica turned the mask around

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:40.680
so it was back-to-front on Trish and teased
her about how she'd fight like that, it felt

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:45.070
like the show was poking a little fun at Daredevil.
There's also a subtle nod to the white and

00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:49.500
blue colours of Jewel's costume in the light
shades in Jessica's apartment block which

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:54.060
are white with a thin blue line around them.
During conversations Jessica has with Luke

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:58.759
Cage and Young Trish, there's mention of Jessica
getting her powers from the car accident which

00:01:58.759 --> 00:02:02.630
killed her biological family.
And the flashback scenes of the car accident

00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:06.479
where Jessica fights with her brother Phillip
which leads to a family argument and a collision

00:02:06.479 --> 00:02:10.440
with a truck are very reminiscent of the same
scenes in Alias issue 22.

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:15.480
In the accident which happens in the comic,
Jessica is exposed to radioactive chemicals.

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:20.430
So, it's interesting that one of the streets
Jessica recites is called Cobalt Lane, perhaps

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:24.819
a nod to Cobalt Chemical, a New York chemical
corporation in Marvel comics.

00:02:24.819 --> 00:02:28.930
By the way, Higgins Drive, which is another
of the 3 streets Jessica recites, could be

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:33.150
a nod to Nigel Higgins a British version of
the Punisher and, of course, the Punisher

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:37.800
will be appearing in Daredevil Season 2.
Oh, and the Mickey Mouse that one of Kilgrave's

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:42.370
men takes into Jessica's former home is a
nod not only to Disney who own Marvel but

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:46.330
also to the comic books and the trip to Disney
World that Jessica's family were taking when

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:47.569
their car crashed.

00:02:47.569 --> 00:02:51.800
When Jessica's photographing Luke Cage in
episode 1, he's wearing his signature comic-book

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:55.709
colours, yellow and blue, in this case a yellow
T-shirt and blue jeans.

00:02:55.709 --> 00:03:00.090
As for Jessica's relationship with Luke and
their first sex scene, that follows the same

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:04.560
story from Alias issue 1.
And in episode 7, when Jessica goes looking

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:09.019
for Luke at his bar, the bartender says to
her that "you love him […] you just wanna

00:03:09.019 --> 00:03:13.370
have his baby", which is a reference to the
comics where Jessica does in fact have a baby

00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:18.019
girl called Danielle with Luke.
In same scene, there's also a nice nod to

00:03:18.019 --> 00:03:22.790
Jessica's flying skills in the comics when
the bartender tells her, "you know what happens

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:28.730
when you burn a bridge, you have to learn
how to swim, or fly". In Alias issue 23 when

00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:33.530
Jessica discovers she can fly for the first
time, she's not able to control it and falls

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:37.459
in the Hudson River where she nearly drowns
before being saved by Thor.

00:03:37.459 --> 00:03:46.310
By the way, Luke Cage gets to say his comic-book
catchphrase "Sweet Christmas" for the first

00:03:46.310 --> 00:03:57.870
time in episode 3 while he's in bed with Jessica
and later again in episode 6 when they are

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:01.076
both searching for Antoine.
In that same scene when the guard dogs arrive,

00:04:01.076 --> 00:04:02.076
Luke says he'll handle the dogs as sharp teeth
are his speciality. This is a nice nod to

00:04:02.076 --> 00:04:03.076
Cottonmouth, who has sharpened teeth and is
one of Luke Cage's antagonists in the comics.

00:04:03.076 --> 00:04:04.110
In terms of Luke's powers, the show alludes
to the origin of these in the comics when

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:07.640
he says to Jessica that he received his powers
as the result of an experiment.

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:12.610
There seem to be more hints about that experiment
when Jessica hands Luke the flash drive which

00:04:12.610 --> 00:04:17.259
Reva buried and Kilgrave made Jessica dig
up, after all, we already know that drive

00:04:17.259 --> 00:04:21.590
contains videos of experiments on children.
Jessica also mentions there are other files

00:04:21.590 --> 00:04:26.380
on there that she didn't look at. Also notice
that the flash drive is yellow, Luke's signature

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:30.710
colour. I'm guessing we get to see what's
on that drive in the upcoming Luke Cage series.

00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:35.440
The fan who approaches Trish and says "I liked
you with your red hair" is a reference back

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:38.530
to her character Patsy Walker in the comics
who had red hair.

00:04:38.530 --> 00:04:43.250
In fact, in episode 11, we get to see the
young Trish wearing a red wig during a flashback

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:48.150
scene, which also features the intro from
her TV show as a child star, "It's Patsy".

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:53.040
And that fan who liked Trish's red hair is
carrying what looks like a modernised version

00:04:53.040 --> 00:05:26.570
of Patsy Walker Vol 1 Issue 26 which was published
in 1950.

00:05:26.570 --> 00:06:04.130
In the show, Trish wants to don a cape and
save the world. And in the episode 11 flashback

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:05.130
scene I just mentioned, the young Trish's
mother tells a hospitalised Jessica Jones

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:06.130
that "I know you must be confused right now,
but everything's going to be ok. Patsy's going

00:06:06.130 --> 00:06:07.130
to save you."
All of which could be a hint that maybe in

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:08.130
the future we'll get to see Trish as Hellcat,
Patsy Walker's comic book alter ego.

00:06:08.130 --> 00:06:09.130
In the comics, Patsy Walker only has a bit
of interaction with Jessica Jones when she

00:06:09.130 --> 00:06:10.130
applies for the job of nanny to Jessica's
daughter Danielle. Actually, it feels like

00:06:10.130 --> 00:06:11.130
Trish in the show has taken on the role of
Carol Danvers, aka Captain Marvel, who is

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:12.130
Jessica's best friend in the comics. Of course,
Captain Marvel is getting her own film in

00:06:12.130 --> 00:06:13.130
the MCU which is why they didn't want her
character in the TV series.

00:06:13.130 --> 00:06:14.130
By the way, although the Baxter haemodialysis
machine does exist in the real world, its

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:15.130
inclusion in Jessica Jones feels like it has
a little added significance as Robert Baxter,

00:06:15.130 --> 00:06:16.130
aka Mad-Dog, is Patsy's former husband in
the comics.

00:06:16.130 --> 00:06:17.130
Kilgrave's alter ego as The Purple Man in
the comics is constantly referenced throughout

00:06:17.130 --> 00:06:18.130
the TV show, for example, in the use of purple
in the opening credits, with purple lighting

00:06:18.130 --> 00:06:19.130
scattered throughout numerous scenes, Kilgrave
wearing a purple shirt in the final 2 episodes,

00:06:19.130 --> 00:06:20.130
and his face popping with purple veins when
he yells 'stop!' in the finale.

00:06:20.130 --> 00:06:21.130
There's also a verbal mention of the colour
purple when Kilgrave's victims are first interviewed.

00:06:21.130 --> 00:06:24.500
One of them says Kilgrave gave him a purple
staff and told him to stick it, which seems

00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:29.180
like a play on the moment in the comics when
the Purple Man goes into a rage and orders

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:30.829
Jessica to kill Daredevil with some choice
words about a rusty pipe and Daredevil's ass!

00:06:30.829 --> 00:06:31.829
As for Kilgrave's name, there's lots of lovely
jokes about that. In episode 3, when Jessica

00:06:31.829 --> 00:06:32.829
goes into a grocery store, you can hear a
guy on the radio say, "Have you ever heard

00:06:32.829 --> 00:06:33.829
of a more made-up name than Kilgrave?" This
is little inside joke as although in the TV

00:06:33.829 --> 00:06:34.829
show, Kilgrave is an alias for Kevin Thompson,
in the comics Zebediah Killgrave is the character's

00:06:34.829 --> 00:06:35.829
real name and his alias is The Purple Man.
The joke continues in episode 9 when Jessica

00:06:35.829 --> 00:06:36.829
taunts Kilgrave about why he picked that name
asking if MurderCorpse was already taken!

00:06:36.829 --> 00:06:37.829
And again, in episode 13, Claire Temple jokes
he should be called SnuffCarcass.

00:06:37.829 --> 00:06:38.829
By the way, I liked the "what doesn't kill
us makes us stranger" quip Jessica makes to

00:06:38.829 --> 00:06:39.829
Simpson. It's actually a line the Joker says
in The Dark Knight, which feels appropriate

00:06:39.829 --> 00:06:40.829
given the psychotic Joker vibe that David
Tennant's Kilgrave gives off.

00:06:40.829 --> 00:06:41.829
And there's even a clever Doctor Who easter
egg referencing David Tennant's time as the

00:06:41.829 --> 00:06:42.829
Tenth Doctor when Jessica says to Kilgrave
in episode 8, "you're not ten anymore!"

00:06:42.829 --> 00:06:43.829
And Kilgrave's words "Send the picture, save
the junkie" are an echo of the often-repeated

00:06:43.829 --> 00:06:44.829
phrase "save the cheerleader, save the world"
from the TV series Heroes.

00:06:44.829 --> 00:06:45.829
And there's a funny Star Wars joke about Obi-Wan
Kenobi and Kilgrave's powers when he and Jessica

00:06:45.829 --> 00:06:46.829
enter the hostage house.

00:06:46.829 --> 00:06:47.829
NYPD cop and former Special Ops agent Will
Simpson is the MCU's version of the comics'

00:06:47.829 --> 00:06:48.829
Frank Simpson, aka Nuke, who underwent a super-soldier
program similar to the one which created Captain

00:06:48.829 --> 00:06:49.829
America.
And there's a strong similarity between the

00:06:49.829 --> 00:08:08.960
look of actor Wil Traval who plays Will Simpson
in Jessica Jones and Chris Evans who plays

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:12.310
Steve Rogers, aka Captain America, in the
MCU.

00:08:12.310 --> 00:08:16.800
In the comics, one of Nuke's distinguishing
features is the American flag he has tattooed

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:21.330
on his face, and there's a cool nod to this
in the show with the American flag on Simpson's

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:24.520
lighter.
Like in the show, in the comics, Nuke is given

00:08:24.520 --> 00:08:28.860
red, white, and blue pills, and he experiences
the same cravings for the red pills which

00:08:28.860 --> 00:08:33.320
have similar effects.
Kozlov who gave him the pills is a bit of

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:38.589
a mystery as there isn't a direct comic-book
counterpart for him, however there is a scientist

00:08:38.589 --> 00:08:43.830
called Michael Kozlov who appeared in the
Strange Tales and Where Monsters Dwell series.

00:08:43.830 --> 00:08:49.899
Later Trish discovers Kozlov works for IGH,
which she says is a mysterious private research

00:08:49.899 --> 00:08:53.130
corporation and she's seen investigating it
at the end of the series.

00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:57.750
And there are hints that the origins of Jessica's
superpowers may have something to do with

00:08:57.750 --> 00:09:02.750
IGH too as Trish's mother Dorothy reveals
they paid for Jessica's medical bills after

00:09:02.750 --> 00:09:06.860
the car accident.
Maybe IGH, which Trish says is 'an acronym

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:14.050
for nothing', is actually an allusion to the
comics' MGH, aka Mutant Growth Hormone. The

00:09:14.050 --> 00:09:20.200
MCU can't reference MGH itself as Fox have
the film rights to mutant-related stuff, but

00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:26.000
perhaps IGH could stand for Inhuman Growth
Hormone, as the Inhumans are already part

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:30.870
of the MCU in both Agents of SHIELD and are
set to have their own movie. I'd love to know

00:09:30.870 --> 00:09:34.550
any theories you've got about what the mysterious
IGH is.

00:09:34.550 --> 00:09:39.130
By the way, it's interesting that when Jessica
is fighting Simpson in her apartment she slams

00:09:39.130 --> 00:09:44.510
him repeatedly into her refrigerator, which
she then pulls on top of him. This feels like

00:09:44.510 --> 00:09:48.870
a deliberate swipe at and reversal with a
twist of the trope that comic book writer

00:09:48.870 --> 00:09:53.480
Gail Simone called "Women in Refrigerators".
Simone drew up a list of female comic book

00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:58.440
characters who have been, as she put it, "depowered,
raped, or cut up and stuck in the refrigerator".

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:03.019
The idea originates from a Green Lantern comic
where Kyle Rayner's girlfriend is murdered

00:10:03.019 --> 00:10:07.630
and stuffed into a refrigerator. By alluding
to this trope, the show seems to be making

00:10:07.630 --> 00:10:12.480
a point about its treatment of female characters
and saying that 'nobody puts Jessica Jones

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:13.720
in a refrigerator.'

00:10:13.720 --> 00:10:18.070
It's interesting that Claire Temple, who we've
already seen in season 1 of Daredevil, plays

00:10:18.070 --> 00:10:21.070
a big part in the final episode of Jessica
Jones.

00:10:21.070 --> 00:10:26.180
It's curious that Claire made a point of saying
she's no one's sidekick while talking to Malcolm.

00:10:26.180 --> 00:10:30.320
Marvel didn't allow the Daredevil series to
use the name Night Nurse for Rosario Dawson's

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:35.240
character Claire as there were potential plans
to use Night Nurse in the movies. Maybe Claire's

00:10:35.240 --> 00:10:39.350
words could therefore hint at bigger plans
ahead for Night Nurse in the MCU.

00:10:39.350 --> 00:10:47.430
Other characters who've been changed from
the comics include Jeryn Hogarth and Malcolm.

00:10:47.430 --> 00:10:52.920
Hogarth the shark-like lawyer played by Carrie-Anne
Moss is actually a male lawyer in the comics

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:57.500
and has a strong connection to Iron Fist.
It'll be interesting to see what role Carrie-Anne

00:10:57.500 --> 00:11:00.540
Moss gets in the Iron Fist series planned
for Netflix.

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:04.950
In the Jessica Jones comics, Malcolm pops
up from time-to-time as an annoying fanboy

00:11:04.950 --> 00:11:08.890
who wants to help out Jessica. In the show
he's a much more significant character with

00:11:08.890 --> 00:11:13.160
new storylines, however the final scene in
the series, where he answers Jessica's phone,

00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:16.480
brings to mind scenes where he does the same
in the Alias comics.

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:21.620
And it wouldn't be a Marvel production without
a cameo from Stan Lee who pops up in a framed

00:11:21.620 --> 00:11:25.230
picture at the police station.
Also pinned up at the police station you can

00:11:25.230 --> 00:11:29.560
spot the newspaper cutting for journalist
Ben Urich's "Union Allied Corruption Scandal"

00:11:29.560 --> 00:11:32.640
article in the New York Bulletin from Daredevil
Season 1.

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:37.820
There's an amusing reference to Grey's Anatomy,
which is on the Disney-owned channel ABC,

00:11:37.820 --> 00:11:43.040
when Jessica pretends to be a nurse and claims
she previously worked at Seattle Grace hospital.

00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:49.220
The title of episode 8, AKA WWJD, is likely
short for "What Would Jessica Do", which is

00:11:49.220 --> 00:11:52.800
a nice play on the usual meaning of the acronym,
"What Would Jesus Do?"

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:57.570
And the yacht named Goldfish that Kilgrave
plans to leave town on is a nice nod to the

00:11:57.570 --> 00:12:02.839
comic book series AKA Goldfish written and
drawn by Brian Michael Bendis who, of course,

00:12:02.839 --> 00:12:06.550
created the character of Jessica Jones with
artist Michael Gaydos.

00:12:06.550 --> 00:12:10.760
Speaking of Bendis and Gaydos, there are more
allusions in the show to their Alias comics.

00:12:10.760 --> 00:12:15.990
Ruben's death in Jessica's bed has a similar
feel to the vision of Scott Lang dead in her

00:12:15.990 --> 00:12:19.750
bed that Kilgrave makes her see in Alias issue
27.

00:12:19.750 --> 00:12:24.260
The Supermax prison Jessica wants to get herself
locked up in is likely the equivalent of the

00:12:24.260 --> 00:12:27.829
comics' Raft prison which is housed on Ryker's
Island.

00:12:27.829 --> 00:12:32.320
When Hogarth says to Jessica that Supermax
is full of "top-shelf perverts, cannibals,

00:12:32.320 --> 00:12:36.930
and serial killers who make mosaics with their
victims' fingernails" these kind of feel like

00:12:36.930 --> 00:12:41.791
allusions to high-powered criminals like Killgrave
himself, Baron Blood, and Jigsaw who were

00:12:41.791 --> 00:12:46.519
all in jail in the Raft in the Alias comics.
And Kilgrave's use of mind control to win

00:12:46.519 --> 00:12:50.120
a card game in episode 6 reminds me of when
he took over a casino in the comic series

00:12:50.120 --> 00:12:51.120
"Super-Villain Team-Up MODOK's 11".
And the final showdown when Kilgrave orders

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:52.120
everyone at the ferry terminal to kill each
other is strikingly similar to a scene in

00:12:52.120 --> 00:12:53.120
the final Alias comic when Killgrave orders
everyone to beat up the person to their left

00:12:53.120 --> 00:12:54.120
until they're dead.

00:12:54.120 --> 00:12:55.120
There are plenty of nods to Marvel's next
movie Captain America: Civil War, with the

00:12:55.120 --> 00:12:56.120
show making various references to how superheroes
fit into society.

00:12:56.120 --> 00:12:57.120
You can sense the conflict that's still brewing
between people with and without superpowers,

00:12:57.120 --> 00:12:58.120
for example, during a radio talk show we hear
when Jessica visits a grocery store, one caller

00:12:58.120 --> 00:12:59.120
talks about there being more sightings of
superheroes since 'those guys quote saved

00:12:59.120 --> 00:13:00.120
the city', and the caller also mentions all
the mess superheroes create for everyone else.

00:13:00.120 --> 00:13:01.120
There's also the subplot with Audrey Eastman
who wants to avenge her mother's death in

00:13:01.120 --> 00:13:02.120
the Battle Of New York so she tricks Jessica
into doing a PI job for her, only to try and

00:13:02.120 --> 00:13:03.120
kill her. As far as Eastman's concerned, people
with powers are freaks, not gifted.

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:04.120
In that same storyline, there are allusions
to specific MCU characters when Jessica mentions

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:05.120
'the big green guy' and 'the flag waver.
Oh, and when Jessica first tries to abduct

00:13:05.120 --> 00:14:11.446
Kilgrave, a kid runs by in the park wearing
a Captain America costume.

00:14:11.446 --> 00:14:12.446
Again, the show's references to revealing
superhero identities fits in with the Civil

00:14:12.446 --> 00:14:15.250
War set-up. For example, in the first episode,
when Jessica lifts up a car, the driver threatens

00:14:15.250 --> 00:14:19.839
to out her. She replies that she's not hiding
and nobody cares anyway as they just want

00:14:19.839 --> 00:14:24.380
to feel safe, though she also adds, 'I'd rather
call you crazy than admit that I can lift

00:14:24.380 --> 00:14:27.459
this car or melt your insides with my laser
eyes'.

00:14:27.459 --> 00:14:32.230
Later, during a conversation with Luke, Jessica
again says she's not hiding but adds she's

00:14:32.230 --> 00:14:37.540
not advertising either. And Luke points out
that 'being a hero just puts a target on your

00:14:37.540 --> 00:14:38.540
back.'

00:14:38.540 --> 00:14:43.130
In other Marvel references, when Jessica recommends
another private investigator to Luke Cage

00:14:43.130 --> 00:14:48.440
she name-drops Angela Del Toro, aka White
Tiger, who appears in the Daredevil comics.

00:14:48.440 --> 00:14:55.389
In episode 8, WHIH World News appears on TV
to report on the hostage standoff. WHIH is

00:14:55.389 --> 00:14:59.949
a Marvel regular and its TV news stories have
appeared in Daredevil, Agents of SHIELD, Avengers:

00:14:59.949 --> 00:15:03.410
Age of Ultron, The Incredible Hulk, and Iron
Man 2.

00:15:03.410 --> 00:15:07.880
And there's another nod to Avengers: Age of
Ultron when Jessica says "go to sleep" when

00:15:07.880 --> 00:15:12.360
she takes out a guy who's attacking her because
Kilgrave's mind-controlling him. In Age Of

00:15:12.360 --> 00:15:18.180
Ultron, Scarlet Witch hexes Bruce Banner's
mind sending him on a rampage and Iron Man

00:15:18.180 --> 00:15:22.620
tries to bring him back by hammering his head
while repeating the words 'go to sleep.'

00:15:22.620 --> 00:15:27.760
In episode 6, Jessica scoffs that the loan
shark Sirkes thinks he's Carl Icahn. Which

00:15:27.760 --> 00:15:32.620
is funny as Carl Icahn is a real-life investor
who once tried to take over Marvel.

00:15:32.620 --> 00:15:38.230
Also Keibler Circus pops up on a sign behind
Wendy in episode 9. Keibler Circus was later

00:15:38.230 --> 00:15:42.970
known as the Tiboldt Circus, and Clint Barton,
aka Hawkeye, and the Hulk, among others, were

00:15:42.970 --> 00:15:47.000
members at various points.
And in episode 10 we hear that Hope's aborted

00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:50.910
foetus which contains Kilgrave's DNA was sent
to Hammond Labs for testing which could be

00:15:50.910 --> 00:15:56.060
the same Hammond Labs from the comics that
Robbie Baldwin, aka Speedball, interned at.

